Touchscreen bring-up follow-up, real-hardware fix (2026-09-20,
docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md "touch debugging" section): the touch
driver added in 0003 worked well enough for a bring-up smoke test but
was unusable under sustained real interactive load - Plasma reported
constant phantom "holding down" state, no swipe-scrolling, and evtest
showed a continuous ~90Hz interrupt storm (regardless of finger
presence) with most reads returning all-0xFF.

Root cause, found by reading Samsung's downstream driver
(references/gts7l/drivers/input/touchscreen/novatek/nt36523/
nt36xxx_fw_update.c) rather than continuing to guess: this NT36523
needs firmware flashed to its internal flash on every single boot
(nvt_ts_firmware_update_on_probe(), called from downstream's own
probe() right after chip-ID, before the IRQ is ever requested) - our
0003 driver never did this at all, on the wrong assumption that real
non-volatile storage meant nothing needed pushing from the host. The
chip was actually stuck in its own bootROM's CRC-fail auto-reboot
loop the whole time; the all-0xFF reads were exactly what that failure
mode looks like at the I2C level (a real ACK, garbage content).

Two fixes landed together:

1. A probe-time nt36523_stop_crc_reboot()/nt36523_in_crc_reboot_loop()
   pair, ported register-for-register from downstream's
   nvt_ts_stop_crc_reboot() - detects and clears the CRC-fail flag
   before anything else runs. This alone was NOT sufficient (the
   underlying missing firmware just let the chip re-enter the same
   state) - documented here because an earlier attempt at also
   re-running this check on every single IRQ was tried and reverted
   (it misfired on ordinary "no active touch" idle reports, which
   naturally read as 0xFF in the point-data buffer for unrelated
   reasons, and made touch stop working entirely rather than fixing
   the choppiness - a real regression caught and undone before landing
   here).

2. The actual fix: nt36523_update_firmware() and its helpers
   (nt36523_erase_flash/nt36523_write_flash/nt36523_verify_flash/etc.),
   ported from nt36xxx_fw_update.c's erase/program/verify sequence,
   run unconditionally at every probe (no version-check-and-skip fast
   path, unlike downstream - deliberately always reflashes rather than
   risk getting a checksum-compare short-circuit subtly wrong on a
   one-of-a-kind piece of hardware). The firmware blob itself is now
   tracked at kernel/firmware/tsp_novatek/nt36523_gts7l.bin (the
   gts7l-specific one, originally sourced from
   references/gts7l/firmware/tsp_novatek/ - a same-named but different
   file also exists under references/kernel_samsung_sm8250/, not used)
   and bundled directly into the kernel binary via CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE
   (this patch's kernel/config/gts7l.fragment addition, copied into
   work/linux/firmware/tsp_novatek/ at build time) rather than relying
   on a userspace /lib/firmware path, so request_firmware() never
   depends on rootfs mount timing.

Confirmed on real hardware: touch report read errors dropped from a
continuous storm to 10 total (all transient, right at boot) across a
full session, and evtest showed a clean, monotonic drag trajectory
with no more phantom reversals/corruption. One known remaining issue,
NOT fixed by this patch and left for later: real position updates
still only land every ~150-700ms even though the IRQ itself still
fires at ~90Hz - live register reads during this behavior directly
ruled out both an obvious low-power/stuck-in-calibration state
(RESET_COMPLETE reads back 0xA3, RESET_STATE_NORMAL_RUN) and an
active noise-avoidance mode (FUNCT_STATE's noise_mode bit reads 0),
and reviewing both Samsung's downstream sec_fn.c and the real,
in-flight mainline NT36xxx driver submission
(github.com/pundiramit/linux, lore.kernel.org/r/
20241015-nt36xxx-v1-2-3919d0bffee6@gmail.com) turned up no documented
"increase report rate" step being missed - this looks like this
chip/firmware's actual default report cadence, not a bug with a known
fix. See docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md for the full investigation.

Also fixed in kernel/dts/sm8250-samsung-gts7l.dts (tracked directly in
this repo, not part of this patch): the touchscreen@62 node never
actually referenced its own already-correctly-defined nvt_ts_int_gts7l
pinctrl state (bias-disable, matching Samsung's downstream attn_irq
node) - missing pinctrl-names/pinctrl-0 properties left GPIO 15 at the
SoC's reset-default bias-pull-down on an active-low IRQ line. Wired up
and confirmed live via /sys/kernel/debug/gpio. This was investigated
as a possible cause of the interrupt storm above and ruled out (storm
rate was identical under pull-down/no-pull/pull-up), but is kept since
it's a real, independently-correct fix matching Samsung's own hardware
configuration.

Applies on top of Linux 7.2 with 0002 and 0003 already applied.

diff --git a/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36523-gts7l.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36523-gts7l.c
index 7e936ca..c006e8c 100644
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36523-gts7l.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36523-gts7l.c
@@ -20,12 +20,20 @@
  *    By the time this probes, the chip is already out of reset and
  *    running with whatever firmware Samsung's factory already flashed
  *    to its internal, persistent flash.
- *  - No firmware download: downstream's own driver has a purpose-built
- *    "bringup" mode that skips firmware flashing entirely
- *    (nvt_ts_firmware_update_on_probe()'s `bringup == 1` check) for
- *    exactly this situation - this chip has real non-volatile storage,
- *    not a RAM-only firmware need, so there's nothing to push from the
- *    host for a first driver to work.
+ *  - Firmware IS flashed at every probe, unconditionally: an earlier
+ *    version of this driver skipped it (mirroring downstream's
+ *    `bringup == 1` mode), on the assumption that this chip's real
+ *    non-volatile flash meant there was nothing to push for a first
+ *    driver to work. Real hardware testing under sustained interactive
+ *    load showed that assumption was wrong - the chip was actually
+ *    stuck in its own bootROM's CRC-fail auto-reboot loop the whole
+ *    time (nvt_ts_stop_crc_reboot() below only ever cleared the
+ *    symptom, never the cause), which is exactly what downstream's
+ *    firmware-update-on-probe step (nvt_ts_firmware_update_on_probe()
+ *    in nt36xxx_fw_update.c) exists to fix. Ported that flash sequence
+ *    (see nt36523_update_firmware() below) rather than continuing to
+ *    special-case around a chip that was simply missing working
+ *    firmware.
  *  - Real protocol is paged/banked I2C addressing, not a match for
  *    mainline's existing novatek-nvt-ts.c (a different, flatly-
  *    addressed Novatek chip family): the "address" the downstream
@@ -37,11 +45,13 @@
  */
 
 #include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/firmware.h>
 #include <linux/i2c.h>
 #include <linux/input.h>
 #include <linux/input/mt.h>
 #include <linux/input/touchscreen.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
+#include <linux/minmax.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 
 /*
@@ -135,6 +145,543 @@ static int nt36523_set_page(struct i2c_client *client, u16 addr, u32 page)
 	return nt36523_i2c_write(client, addr, buf, sizeof(buf));
 }
 
+/*
+ * gts7l (docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): under real interactive load
+ * (not just the bring-up smoke test), this chip was found firing its
+ * IRQ line continuously (~90Hz, regardless of finger presence) with
+ * every read returning all-0xFF - and neither the GPIO's pull
+ * configuration nor re-selecting the paged register window on every
+ * read changed that at all, ruling out an electrical/floating-line
+ * explanation. Downstream's own driver
+ * (nvt_ts_check_chip_ver_trim()/nvt_ts_stop_crc_reboot() in nt36xxx.c)
+ * checks for exactly this pattern (buf[1]==0xFC, or
+ * buf[1]==buf[2]==buf[3]==0xFF) at a specific register and identifies
+ * it explicitly as "IC is in CRC fail reboot loop" - this chip has an
+ * internal firmware-integrity auto-reboot feature that, once it starts
+ * misfiring, needs to be actively stopped by the host; it doesn't clear
+ * itself. Ported downstream's exact recovery sequence (same page/offset
+ * values, same reset-idle + CRC_ERR_FLAG-clear writes) rather than
+ * guessing at a new one - this is real, vendor-confirmed chip behavior,
+ * not something to reverse-engineer from scratch.
+ */
+#define NT36523_CRC_CHECK_PAGE		0x01f600
+#define NT36523_CRC_CHECK_OFFSET	0x4e
+#define NT36523_CRC_CLEAR_PAGE		0x03f100
+#define NT36523_CRC_CLEAR_OFFSET	0x35
+#define NT36523_CRC_STOP_RETRIES	5
+
+static bool nt36523_in_crc_reboot_loop(struct i2c_client *client)
+{
+	u8 buf[4];
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = nt36523_set_page(client, NT36523_I2C_FW_ADDR,
+				NT36523_CRC_CHECK_PAGE);
+	if (ret)
+		return false;
+
+	buf[0] = NT36523_CRC_CHECK_OFFSET;
+	ret = nt36523_i2c_read(client, NT36523_I2C_FW_ADDR, buf, sizeof(buf));
+	if (ret)
+		return false;
+
+	return buf[1] == 0xfc || (buf[1] == 0xff && buf[2] == 0xff && buf[3] == 0xff);
+}
+
+static int nt36523_stop_crc_reboot(struct i2c_client *client)
+{
+	u8 buf[2];
+	int ret;
+	int i;
+
+	if (!nt36523_in_crc_reboot_loop(client))
+		return 0;
+
+	dev_warn(&client->dev,
+		 "chip is in a CRC fail auto-reboot loop, stopping it\n");
+
+	for (i = 0; i < NT36523_CRC_STOP_RETRIES; i++) {
+		/* Reset-idle, written twice (matches downstream exactly). */
+		buf[0] = 0x00;
+		buf[1] = 0xa5;
+		nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, sizeof(buf));
+		nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, sizeof(buf));
+		mdelay(1);
+
+		ret = nt36523_set_page(client, NT36523_I2C_FW_ADDR,
+					NT36523_CRC_CLEAR_PAGE);
+		if (ret)
+			continue;
+
+		buf[0] = NT36523_CRC_CLEAR_OFFSET;
+		buf[1] = 0xa5;
+		nt36523_i2c_write(client, NT36523_I2C_FW_ADDR, buf, sizeof(buf));
+
+		ret = nt36523_set_page(client, NT36523_I2C_FW_ADDR,
+					NT36523_CRC_CLEAR_PAGE);
+		if (ret)
+			continue;
+
+		buf[0] = NT36523_CRC_CLEAR_OFFSET;
+		ret = nt36523_i2c_read(client, NT36523_I2C_FW_ADDR, buf, 2);
+		if (!ret && buf[1] == 0xa5)
+			return 0;
+	}
+
+	dev_err(&client->dev, "failed to stop CRC auto-reboot loop\n");
+	return -ETIMEDOUT;
+}
+
+/*
+ * gts7l (docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): the CRC-reboot condition above
+ * turned out to be a symptom, not the disease. Downstream's own driver
+ * (nt36xxx_fw_update.c) unconditionally flashes firmware to this chip's
+ * internal flash on every single boot, before ever requesting the IRQ
+ * (nvt_ts_firmware_update_on_probe(), called from nvt_ts_probe() right
+ * after chip-ID). This mainline driver never did that at all - the chip
+ * was limping along on whatever was left in its flash, which is exactly
+ * what an NT36523 bootROM's own CRC-fail auto-reboot condition looks
+ * like when the app firmware doesn't verify. The fix is to actually push
+ * firmware, the same way downstream does: erase, program, verify, then
+ * a bootloader reset and wait for the chip to report RESET_STATE_INIT.
+ * Ported register-for-register from nt36xxx_fw_update.c rather than
+ * re-deriving it - this is real, vendor-confirmed protocol, not
+ * something to guess at. Unlike downstream, there's no
+ * check-version-then-skip fast path here: this always reflashes on
+ * every boot, trading a couple of extra seconds at probe time for not
+ * having to get a checksum-compare short-circuit bug-for-bug right on a
+ * one-of-a-kind piece of hardware.
+ */
+#define NT36523_FW_NAME		"tsp_novatek/nt36523_gts7l.bin"
+
+/* RW_FLASH_DATA_ADDR / READ_FLASH_CHECKSUM_ADDR from the NT36523 entry
+ * in references/gts7l/.../nt36xxx_mem_map.h (same table EVENT_BUF_ADDR
+ * above came from).
+ */
+#define NT36523_RW_FLASH_DATA_ADDR	0x024002
+#define NT36523_READ_FLASH_CHECKSUM_ADDR 0x024000
+
+#define NT36523_FLASH_SECTOR_SIZE	4096
+#define NT36523_FLASH_END_FLAG_LEN	3
+#define NT36523_FLASH_BLOCK_SIZE	65536
+#define NT36523_FLASH_BLOCK_COUNT	4
+
+#define NT36523_EVENT_MAP_RESET_COMPLETE 0x60
+#define NT36523_RESET_STATE_INIT	0xa0
+#define NT36523_RESET_STATE_MAX	0xaf
+
+static void nt36523_sw_reset_idle(struct i2c_client *client)
+{
+	u8 buf[2] = { 0x00, 0xa5 };
+
+	nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, sizeof(buf));
+	msleep(15);
+}
+
+static void nt36523_bootloader_reset(struct i2c_client *client)
+{
+	u8 buf[2] = { 0x00, 0x69 };
+
+	nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, sizeof(buf));
+	msleep(35);
+}
+
+static int nt36523_check_fw_reset_state(struct i2c_client *client,
+					  u8 reset_state)
+{
+	u8 buf[6];
+	int ret;
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i <= 100; i++) {
+		msleep(10);
+
+		buf[0] = NT36523_EVENT_MAP_RESET_COMPLETE;
+		ret = nt36523_i2c_read(client, NT36523_I2C_FW_ADDR, buf,
+					sizeof(buf));
+		if (!ret && buf[1] >= reset_state &&
+		    buf[1] <= NT36523_RESET_STATE_MAX)
+			return 0;
+	}
+
+	return -ETIMEDOUT;
+}
+
+/* Polls the 2-byte "0xAA" bootloader command-ack pattern used by nearly
+ * every flash step below.
+ */
+static int nt36523_poll_ack(struct i2c_client *client, unsigned int retries,
+			     unsigned int delay_ms)
+{
+	u8 buf[2];
+	int ret;
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < retries; i++) {
+		mdelay(delay_ms);
+
+		buf[0] = 0x00;
+		ret = nt36523_i2c_read(client, client->addr, buf, 2);
+		if (!ret && buf[1] == 0xaa)
+			return 0;
+	}
+
+	return -ETIMEDOUT;
+}
+
+/* "Read Status" step: write the read-status command, then poll for
+ * buf[1]==0xAA && buf[2]==0x00 (idle/ready, no error).
+ */
+static int nt36523_wait_flash_status(struct i2c_client *client,
+				      unsigned int retries)
+{
+	u8 buf[3];
+	int ret;
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < retries; i++) {
+		mdelay(5);
+
+		buf[0] = 0x00;
+		buf[1] = 0x05;
+		ret = nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, 2);
+		if (ret)
+			continue;
+
+		buf[0] = 0x00;
+		ret = nt36523_i2c_read(client, client->addr, buf, 3);
+		if (!ret && buf[1] == 0xaa && buf[2] == 0x00)
+			return 0;
+	}
+
+	return -ETIMEDOUT;
+}
+
+static int nt36523_init_bootloader(struct i2c_client *client)
+{
+	u8 buf[3];
+	int ret;
+
+	nt36523_sw_reset_idle(client);
+
+	buf[0] = 0x00;
+	buf[1] = 0x00;
+	buf[2] = NT36523_I2C_FW_ADDR;
+	ret = nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, 3);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = nt36523_poll_ack(client, 20, 1);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	msleep(20);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int nt36523_resume_pd(struct i2c_client *client)
+{
+	u8 buf[2] = { 0x00, 0xab };
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, sizeof(buf));
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = nt36523_poll_ack(client, 20, 1);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	msleep(10);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int nt36523_erase_flash(struct i2c_client *client, size_t write_size)
+{
+	u8 buf[8];
+	int ret;
+	unsigned int count, i;
+
+	buf[0] = 0x00;
+	buf[1] = 0x06;
+	ret = nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, 2);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	ret = nt36523_poll_ack(client, 20, 1);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	buf[0] = 0x00;
+	buf[1] = 0x01;
+	buf[2] = 0x00;
+	ret = nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, 3);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	ret = nt36523_poll_ack(client, 20, 1);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = nt36523_wait_flash_status(client, 100);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	count = DIV_ROUND_UP(write_size, NT36523_FLASH_SECTOR_SIZE);
+	for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {
+		u32 addr = i * NT36523_FLASH_SECTOR_SIZE;
+
+		buf[0] = 0x00;
+		buf[1] = 0x06;
+		ret = nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, 2);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+		ret = nt36523_poll_ack(client, 20, 1);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		buf[0] = 0x00;
+		buf[1] = 0x20;
+		buf[2] = (addr >> 16) & 0xff;
+		buf[3] = (addr >> 8) & 0xff;
+		buf[4] = addr & 0xff;
+		ret = nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, 5);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+		ret = nt36523_poll_ack(client, 20, 1);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		ret = nt36523_wait_flash_status(client, 100);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int nt36523_write_flash(struct i2c_client *client, const u8 *data,
+				size_t write_size)
+{
+	u8 buf[34];
+	int ret;
+	unsigned int count, i, j, k;
+
+	ret = nt36523_set_page(client, NT36523_I2C_FW_ADDR,
+				NT36523_RW_FLASH_DATA_ADDR);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	count = DIV_ROUND_UP(write_size, 256);
+	for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {
+		u32 addr = i * 256;
+		size_t page_len = min_t(size_t, write_size - addr, 256);
+		unsigned int csum;
+		u8 status;
+
+		buf[0] = 0x00;
+		buf[1] = 0x06;
+		ret = nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, 2);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+		ret = nt36523_poll_ack(client, 20, 1);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		for (j = 0; j < page_len; j += 32) {
+			size_t chunk = min_t(size_t, page_len - j, 32);
+
+			buf[0] = (NT36523_RW_FLASH_DATA_ADDR + j) & 0xff;
+			for (k = 0; k < chunk; k++)
+				buf[1 + k] = data[addr + j + k];
+
+			ret = nt36523_i2c_write(client, NT36523_I2C_FW_ADDR,
+						 buf, 1 + chunk);
+			if (ret)
+				return ret;
+		}
+
+		if (write_size - addr >= 256)
+			csum = (addr >> 16) + ((addr >> 8) & 0xff) +
+			       (addr & 0xff) + 255;
+		else
+			csum = (addr >> 16) + ((addr >> 8) & 0xff) +
+			       (addr & 0xff) + (write_size - addr - 1);
+		for (k = 0; k < page_len; k++)
+			csum += data[addr + k];
+		csum = (u8)(255 - csum + 1);
+
+		buf[0] = 0x00;
+		buf[1] = 0x02;
+		buf[2] = (addr >> 16) & 0xff;
+		buf[3] = (addr >> 8) & 0xff;
+		buf[4] = addr & 0xff;
+		buf[5] = 0x00;
+		buf[6] = page_len - 1;
+		buf[7] = csum;
+		ret = nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, 8);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		ret = nt36523_poll_ack(client, 20, 1);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+		buf[0] = 0x00;
+		ret = nt36523_i2c_read(client, client->addr, buf, 2);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+		status = buf[1];
+		if (status == 0xea)
+			return -EIO;
+
+		ret = nt36523_wait_flash_status(client, 100);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int nt36523_verify_flash(struct i2c_client *client, const u8 *data,
+				 size_t write_size)
+{
+	u8 buf[8];
+	int ret;
+	unsigned int i, k;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < NT36523_FLASH_BLOCK_COUNT; i++) {
+		u32 block_off = i * NT36523_FLASH_BLOCK_SIZE;
+		size_t len_in_blk;
+		u16 wr_csum, rd_csum;
+
+		if (write_size <= block_off)
+			continue;
+
+		len_in_blk = min_t(size_t, write_size - block_off,
+				    NT36523_FLASH_BLOCK_SIZE);
+
+		wr_csum = i + (((len_in_blk - 1) >> 8) & 0xff) +
+			  ((len_in_blk - 1) & 0xff);
+		for (k = 0; k < len_in_blk; k++)
+			wr_csum += data[block_off + k];
+		wr_csum = 65535 - wr_csum + 1;
+
+		buf[0] = 0x00;
+		buf[1] = 0x07;
+		buf[2] = i;
+		buf[3] = 0x00;
+		buf[4] = 0x00;
+		buf[5] = ((len_in_blk - 1) >> 8) & 0xff;
+		buf[6] = (len_in_blk - 1) & 0xff;
+		ret = nt36523_i2c_write(client, client->addr, buf, 7);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		ret = nt36523_poll_ack(client, 5, 80);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		ret = nt36523_set_page(client, NT36523_I2C_FW_ADDR,
+					NT36523_READ_FLASH_CHECKSUM_ADDR);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		buf[0] = NT36523_READ_FLASH_CHECKSUM_ADDR & 0xff;
+		ret = nt36523_i2c_read(client, NT36523_I2C_FW_ADDR, buf, 3);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		rd_csum = (buf[2] << 8) | buf[1];
+		if (wr_csum != rd_csum) {
+			dev_err(&client->dev,
+				"firmware verify failed on block %u\n", i);
+			return -EIO;
+		}
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/* Scans backward from the end of the file for the "NVT" 3-byte flag
+ * that marks the real end of firmware content in this .bin (there's
+ * padding after it) - references/gts7l/.../nt36xxx_fw_update.c's
+ * nvt_get_fw_need_write_size().
+ */
+static int nt36523_fw_need_write_size(const struct firmware *fw,
+				       size_t *write_size)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = fw->size / NT36523_FLASH_SECTOR_SIZE; i > 0; i--) {
+		size_t off = i * NT36523_FLASH_SECTOR_SIZE -
+			     NT36523_FLASH_END_FLAG_LEN;
+
+		if (!memcmp(&fw->data[off], "NVT", NT36523_FLASH_END_FLAG_LEN)) {
+			*write_size = i * NT36523_FLASH_SECTOR_SIZE;
+			return 0;
+		}
+	}
+
+	return -EINVAL;
+}
+
+static int nt36523_flash_firmware(struct i2c_client *client,
+				   const struct firmware *fw)
+{
+	size_t write_size;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = nt36523_fw_need_write_size(fw, &write_size);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(&client->dev, "firmware end flag not found\n");
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	nt36523_stop_crc_reboot(client);
+
+	ret = nt36523_init_bootloader(client);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = nt36523_resume_pd(client);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = nt36523_erase_flash(client, write_size);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = nt36523_write_flash(client, fw->data, write_size);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = nt36523_verify_flash(client, fw->data, write_size);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	nt36523_bootloader_reset(client);
+
+	return nt36523_check_fw_reset_state(client, NT36523_RESET_STATE_INIT);
+}
+
+static int nt36523_update_firmware(struct i2c_client *client)
+{
+	const struct firmware *fw;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = request_firmware(&fw, NT36523_FW_NAME, &client->dev);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(&client->dev, "failed to load firmware %s: %d\n",
+			NT36523_FW_NAME, ret);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	ret = nt36523_flash_firmware(client, fw);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_err(&client->dev, "firmware flash failed: %d\n", ret);
+
+	release_firmware(fw);
+	return ret;
+}
